  {"question": "According to arXiv 2503.23056, how does the CSEP-aware model address both high-effort underprivileged females and overall demographic disparities, and what empirical evidence supports this?", "answer": "The CSEP-aware model allocates positive outcomes proportionally to effort among high-effort underprivileged females, nearly doubling the female-to-male PPR ratio, and mitigates both overall and subgroup disparities. Empirical results show PPR for underprivileged females increases from below 0.3 to nearly 0.7 as effort increases.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "CSEP allocates positive outcomes proportionally to effort among high-effort underprivileged females (≥ mean effort), nearly doubling the female-to-male PPR ratio (0.98 to 2.1) and mitigating both overall demographic disparities and those observed among protected underprivileged subgroups (Fig. 2)."}, {"page": 7, "quote": "Figure 5: Comparison of our CSEP-aware model against EP, DP, and CDP -aware models in distributing positive action towards underprivileged females and males. Higher PPR ratio indicates higher preference of positive action for females."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2503.23056, what is the process for identifying the source of privilege (𝑋𝑝) in AI fairness-aware models, and why is this important?", "answer": "The process involves training a predictor on the nonprotected population and using feature importance to find the unprotected ordinal or numerical attribute with the highest impact on privileged predictions. This helps quantify privilege hierarchies for fairness interventions.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "One simple strategy would be to identify an unprotected attribute which is important in producing favourable outcome for a non-protected demography (historically known to garner privilege), and inherently with its attribute values can segregate the population into different sections of hierarchy."}, {"page": 9, "quote": "step2: Among the subset of unprotected ordinal (𝑋𝑜) e.g., \"Education\" [5], and numerical (𝑋𝑛) e.g., \"Capital gain\" [70] attributes, using a feature importance method F I [28], identify the attribute 𝑋𝑝 ∈ (𝑋𝑜 ⋃ 𝑋𝑛) that has the highest impact on model's predictions for the privileged population D𝑔, i.e., 𝑋𝑝 = argmax 𝑋𝑖 ∈ (𝑋𝑜 ⋃ 𝑋𝑛 ) FI (ℎ(𝑋 ), 𝑋𝑖 )."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2503.23056, what is the definition of indirect discrimination under EU law?", "answer": "Indirect discrimination exists when a neutral provision, criterion or practice disadvantages people with a protected characteristic compared to others, unless justified by a legitimate aim and appropriate means.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "indirect discrimination 12 exists when an ostensibly neutral provision, criterion or practice sets people with a particular protected characteristic in a disadvantaged position compared with others unless that provision, criterion or practice is objectively justified by a legitimate aim and the means of achieving that aim are appropriate and necessary 13 [29, 30]."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2503.23056, which organizations are responsible for developing AI standards under the AI Act, and what is their role?", "answer": "CEN and CENELEC are permanent advisory forum members for the AI Act, tasked with preparing European standards and standardization deliverables, including AI conformity assessment and risk management.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "the European Committee for Standardization (CEN) and the European Committee for Electrotechnical Standardization (CENELEC) are considered permanent members of the advisory forum for the regulation, as specified in Article 67 of the AI Act. As the practical application of the AI Act will rely on the development and implementation of harmonized standards [15], in response to request [27] by the European Commission, CEN and CENELEC accepted to prepare a work programme of European standards and European standardization deliverables [66]."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2503.23056, which attribute is used as a proxy for measuring individual effort in the empirical evaluation?", "answer": "Working hours per week is used as a reference proxy for individual effort in the empirical evaluation.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "Work hours in this example is just used as a reference and not necessarily our viewpoint. In reality measuring effort [13] can vary depending on policy. Our SEP and CSEP notions can comply with any such real-valued function measuring effort."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2503.23056, what is the main limitation of the empirical evaluation regarding the generalizability of the SEP framework?", "answer": "The empirical evaluation is limited to the Adult dataset, so future work should test the framework on additional datasets to ensure generalizability.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "The empirical evaluation is currently limited to Adult, a commonly used benchmark in fairness research. Future work should test the proposed framework on additional datasets representing diverse fairness scenarios to ensure its generalizability across different contexts."}]}
]}
